@*
    One roster picker, used everywhere a *slot* is filled.

    Not yet everywhere a name is chosen: SegmentBuilder still renders its own inline lists,
    because a segment cast is a multi-select rather than a set of slots and the two want
    different shapes. Five places became two, not one — worth saying, because the first
    version of this comment claimed one.

    Before this, the match builder rendered the roster four separate times, inline and
    simultaneously — corner A, A's partners, corner B, B's partners — with no way to
    collapse a list once it had been used. Picking A did not shrink A's list; it added
    another list below it. A UX review measured step 1 of a tag booking at roughly 116
    pick cards, about 12,000px, seventeen phone screens; at three a side and a seventy-name
    roster it is nearer thirty thousand. And because every improvement to selection had to
    be made in four places (five, with the segment builder), none of them were ever made:
    no keyboard, no recency, no relevance, and a single shared search box so that typing to
    find B's partner re-filtered corner A's list and reflowed the page underneath you.

    So: a sheet, opened from a slot, closed on choose. One list at a time.

    Two rules that are the point rather than decoration:

      • **Rows carry the consequence, not the stats.** A row says "Upper card · 4th meeting,
        61% of what the first drew", not "Pop 78 · Cha 3.4". The game already computes all
        of that and showed it three wizard steps later, after the pairing was made and the
        sunk cost of going back was 116 cards of scrolling.

      • **Names already booked stay visible.** Hiding them is what forces the re-scroll, and
        it hides the mistake. They are marked, and tapping one swaps the two slots.
*@
@inherits StateComponent
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@implements IAsyncDisposable

<div class="sheet" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-label="@Title"
     @onkeydown="OnKey" @onkeydown:preventDefault="@SwallowsKey" tabindex="-1" @ref="sheet">
    <div class="sheet__head">
        <div class="sheet__title">
            <span class="label">@Title</span>
            @if (Current is { } filled) { <span class="badge badge--gold">@filled.RingName</span> }
        </div>
        @if (Current is not null)
        {
            <button class="btn btn--ghost btn--sm" @onclick="Clear">Clear</button>
        }
        <button class="btn btn--icon btn--ghost" @onclick="Cancel" aria-label="Close">✕</button>
    </div>

    @{
        // Ranked once per render and shared by the placeholder count, the keyboard cursor
        // and the list. It used to be called twice — the placeholder ran the full ranking
        // again just to say how many names it produced.
        var rows  = Rows();
        var bands = rows.GroupBy(r => BookingSuggestions.Heading(r.Band, r.Wrestler)).ToList();
        int emitted = -1;
    }

    <div class="sheet__filters">
        @if (divisionUntouched && division is { } opened)
        {
            <div class="sheet__default">
                Filtered to the @(opened == Division.Womens ? "women's" : "men's") division, to
                match who is already booked. Tap <strong>All</strong> for an intergender match.
            </div>
        }
        <CastFilter Query="@query" QueryChanged="@OnQuery"
                    Division="@division" DivisionChanged="@(v => { division = v; divisionUntouched = false; cursor = 0; })"
                    HomeBrand="@State.BookingBrand"
                    IncludeCrossover="@includeCrossover"
                    IncludeCrossoverChanged="@(v => { includeCrossover = v; cursor = 0; })"
                    Placeholder="@($"Search {rows.Count} names…")" />
    </div>

    <div class="sheet__body">
        @if (bands.Count == 0)
        {
            <div class="empty">Nobody matches that search.</div>
        }
        else
        {
            @* `index` counts through the rendered order, not through the ranked list.
               It used to be `rows.IndexOf(row)` against the flat ranking while the DOM was
               built from a `GroupBy` — two orders that agree only while the ranking already
               arrives grouped. It stopped arriving grouped, and the cursor pointed 23 rows
               away from the highlight: arrow keys scrolled to one name and Enter booked
               another, off screen. Counting the rows as they are emitted cannot desync,
               whatever a future ranking does. *@
            @foreach (var band in bands)
            {
                <div class="group-label @BookingSuggestions.Tone(band.First().Band)">@band.Key</div>
                @foreach (var row in band)
                {
                    var w = row.Wrestler;

                    // `index` must be a *per-iteration* local. The previous version
                    // incremented one method-scope variable declared outside both loops, and
                    // every row's `@onfocus` lambda closed over that one variable — so all
                    // 76 handlers read the value it held after rendering finished, which is
                    // the last row. Six Tab presses put focus on row 1 and the gold highlight
                    // on row 75, and Enter booked an enhancement talent seventy-five rows
                    // away. That is the exact invariant the comment below says this exists to
                    // protect, broken by the fix for the previous version of it.
                    var index = ++emitted;
                    @* The cursor follows focus rather than the mouse. It used to be an
                       integer that `@onmouseover` also wrote, so the gold highlight and the
                       DOM focus ring were two independent things: Tab to one row, hover
                       another, press Enter, and you booked the one you were not looking at.
                       On a laptop the mouse resting anywhere over the list silently
                       overrode arrow-key navigation. Hover no longer moves it; clicking
                       still books whatever you clicked. *@
                    <button class="prow @(index == cursor ? "is-cursor" : "") @(row.BookedAs is null ? "" : "is-used")"
                            @onclick="() => Choose(w)"
                            @onfocus="() => cursor = index">
                        <span class="prow__name">
                            @w.RingName
                            @* Face or heel, on every row, because it is half of what makes a
                               pairing work and the list showed no trace of it. A word as well
                               as a colour: red and green are the two hues most likely to be
                               the same hue to whoever is reading. *@
                            @if (w.Gimmick is { } g)
                            {
                                <span class="badge badge--sm @g.NaturalAlignment.Badge()">@g.NaturalAlignment.Label()</span>
                            }
                            @* And which division, but only while the list holds both. Once the
                               filter is on one of them the badge is on every row and says
                               nothing — a label that never varies is furniture. *@
                            @if (division is null)
                            {
                                <span class="badge badge--sm @w.Division.Badge()">@w.Division.Label()</span>
                            }
                            @* `badge--dim` was never defined in the stylesheet, so this rendered
                               as a bare outline for the entire life of the picker. *@
                            @if (row.BookedAs is { } role) { <span class="badge badge--sm badge--muted">@role</span> }
                            @if (State.IsCrossover(w))
                            {
                                <span class="badge badge--sm badge--cost">crossover −@BrandIntegrity.CostOf(w).ToString("F1")</span>
                            }
                        </span>
                        <span class="prow__why @BookingSuggestions.Tone(row.Band)">@row.Reason</span>
                        @* Said where the booker is choosing, not three steps later on the
                           confirm button. A tired or rusty wrestler is still bookable — this
                           is a reading, not a gate. *@
                        @if (row.Condition is { } condition)
                        {
                            <span class="prow__cond">⚠ @condition</span>
                        }
                        <span class="prow__pop tabnum @w.CardPosition.Tone()">@w.OvernessDisplay</span>
                        <Trend Momentum="@w.Momentum" ShowLabel="false" />
                    </button>
                }
            }
        }
    </div>
</div>
<div class="sheet__scrim" @onclick="Cancel"></div>

@code {
    /// <summary>What this slot is, for the sheet's heading. "Corner A", "A's partner 2".</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Title { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>Whoever is in the slot now, so the sheet can show it and offer to clear it.</summary>
    [Parameter] public Wrestler? Current { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Names already booked into this match, and what they were booked as. Kept visible and
    /// labelled rather than filtered out — see the file comment.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Func<Wrestler, string?> UsedAs { get; set; } = _ => null;

    /// <summary>
    /// The other side of the match, when it is known. Drives the whole relevance sort: who
    /// has heat with these people, and who the crowd is sick of watching them fight.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<Wrestler> Against { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>
    /// Which division the sheet opens filtered to, or null for the whole roster.
    ///
    /// A *default*, not a gate: the division chips sit right above the list and override it,
    /// so an intergender match is one tap rather than something the picker has decided
    /// against. <see cref="RosterFilters.DivisionFor"/> is the rule that chooses it.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public Division? DefaultDivision { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public EventCallback<Wrestler> Picked { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback Cancelled { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Empties the slot. The old partner pickers toggled off on a second tap.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback Cleared { get; set; }

    private string query = "";
    private Division? division;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the division filter is still the one the sheet opened with. Drives the one
    /// line of explanation below the chips — a filter the booker did not set is one they
    /// have to be told about, or a name they know is on the roster is simply missing and the
    /// picker looks broken.
    /// </summary>
    private bool divisionUntouched = true;

    /// <summary>
    /// The sheet is a fresh component every time it opens, so this runs once per open and
    /// the filter cannot carry over from the slot before it.
    /// </summary>
    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        base.OnInitialized();
        division = DefaultDivision;
    }

    private bool includeCrossover;
    private int cursor;

    private ElementReference sheet;
    private bool focused;

    /// <summary>
    /// Focus moves into the sheet as soon as it opens.
    ///
    /// Without this, `document.activeElement` stayed on the slot button *outside* the
    /// dialog, so Escape did nothing until the user Tabbed in — and that took four Tabs
    /// through background controls, because the sheet is last in DOM order. The README
    /// documents "Escape closes"; it did not, from the state anybody is actually in.
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender && !focused)
        {
            focused = true;
            await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("wrestleSim.lockScroll", true);
            await sheet.FocusAsync();
        }
        else if (moveTo is { } index)
        {
            moveTo = null;
            // Scroll the cursor row into view. Focus stays on the sheet, which owns the key
            // handling — moving DOM focus per row would fight the sheet's own keydown.
            await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("wrestleSim.revealRow", sheet, index);
        }
    }

    private int? moveTo;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether this key is ours. The arrows scrolled the page *behind* the modal — measured
    /// at 0 → 531px while the cursor walked off the bottom of the list — because nothing
    /// called preventDefault.
    /// </summary>
    private bool SwallowsKey => swallow;
    private bool swallow;

    private sealed record Row(Wrestler Wrestler, string Section, string Why, string? UsedAs, int Rank);

    private async Task Cancel()   { await Release(); await Cancelled.InvokeAsync(); }
    private async Task Clear()    { await Release(); await Cleared.InvokeAsync(); }
    private async Task Choose(Wrestler w) { await Release(); await Picked.InvokeAsync(w); }

    /// <summary>Give the page back its scrollbar. Called on every way out of the sheet.</summary>
    private async Task Release()
    {
        if (!focused) return;
        focused = false;
        await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("wrestleSim.lockScroll", false);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// And on disposal, so a sheet torn down by a navigation — which is how F1 got out —
    /// cannot leave the page permanently unscrollable.
    ///
    /// `Dispose()` has to be called by hand. When a component implements both interfaces
    /// Blazor runs **only** the async one, and the base `StateComponent.Dispose()` is the
    /// single place that unsubscribes from `GameState.Changed`. Adding `IAsyncDisposable`
    /// here therefore silently stopped the base cleanup: review measured the subscriber
    /// count climbing 2 → 9 across eight picker opens, each dead component still being
    /// notified of every state change and still holding its captured graph alive.
    /// </summary>
    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await Release();
        Dispose();
    }

    private void OnQuery(string v) { query = v; cursor = 0; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Arrow keys move the highlight, Enter takes it, Escape closes. Ten lines, and the app
    /// had none of it anywhere — a grep for `onkeydown` across every screen returned nothing.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task OnKey(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        // The same order the list renders in — see Rows().
        var rows = Rows();
        swallow = e.Key is "ArrowDown" or "ArrowUp";

        switch (e.Key)
        {
            // Moving the cursor moves *focus*, which scrolls the row into view for free and
            // keeps the highlight and the focus ring as one thing rather than two.
            case "ArrowDown":
                moveTo = Math.Min(cursor + 1, Math.Max(0, rows.Count - 1));
                cursor = moveTo.Value;
                break;

            case "ArrowUp":
                moveTo = Math.Max(cursor - 1, 0);
                cursor = moveTo.Value;
                break;

            case "Enter":
                if (cursor < rows.Count) await Choose(rows[cursor].Wrestler);
                break;

            case "Escape":
                await Cancel();
                break;
        }
    }

    private DateOnly? Today => State.Career?.CurrentDate;

    /// <summary>
    /// The pool, ranked, **in the order the list is rendered**.
    ///
    /// The rows are grouped by band heading for display, so the grouped order is the order a
    /// user sees — and it is now the only order this component has. Two rounds of review
    /// went on this: first the cursor indexed the flat ranking while the DOM came from a
    /// `GroupBy`, and when that was fixed by counting rows as they were emitted, `OnKey`'s
    /// Enter was still resolving `rows[cursor]` against the flat list. The desync moved from
    /// (scroll vs highlight) to (highlight vs Enter), which is worse — the highlighted row
    /// scrolls into view correctly and Enter books something else, with no visual cue at all.
    ///
    /// Flattening the grouping here removes the second order rather than keeping the two in
    /// step, so there is nothing left to desync. The razor still groups, but grouping an
    /// already-grouped list preserves it.
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<BookingSuggestions.Suggestion> Rows() =>
        Ranked().GroupBy(r => BookingSuggestions.Heading(r.Band, r.Wrestler))
                .SelectMany(g => g)
                .ToList();

    private IReadOnlyList<BookingSuggestions.Suggestion> Ranked() =>
        BookingSuggestions.Rank(
            State.Roster
                 .Where(w => includeCrossover || !State.IsCrossover(w))
                 .Where(w => division is null || w.Division == division)
                 .Where(w => Matches(w, query)),
            Against, State.FeudBook, UsedAs, State.StandingPartnerOf, State.RecentlyBooked, Today);

    private static bool Matches(Wrestler w, string q) =>
        string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(q)
        || (w.RingName?.Contains(q, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase) ?? false)
        || (w.RealName?.Contains(q, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase) ?? false);

}
